WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.780 --> 00:00:03.300
You know, when I was six I stuck a fork

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:04.820
into an electrical outlet because I was curious.

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:10.860
I think that that was the
beginning of exploring the force that

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:15.560
was outside myself that could have physical effects on me and my brain and my reality.

00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:20.040
I think Andrew's work blends
this beautiful use of media and

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:26.100
technology and design into these
exciting moments of storytelling.

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:28.980
"Time is Nature's way of keeping

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:30.980
everything from happening all at once."

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:33.580
I'm Andrew Schneider. I'm a director,

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:36.780
designer, performer, writer of NERVOUS/SYSTEM,

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:39.300
which has been developed here at MIT CAST.

00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:46.620
Being here at MIT has been different
than other places we've been to.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:50.160
Most places are venues that we load in and out of.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:52.380
They program a season and we come,

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:55.040
we do the work and we load out.

00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:58.400
Being here is sort of more of a research laboratory.

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:00.380
We're thinking about things
differently here.

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:02.420
When I think about the work,

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:06.420
work I think about the work in not just
this show but about a trajectory of shows.

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:08.640
And so our first show that we made
sort of explores

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:11.540
parallel universes and perception.

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:15.640
And then a second show
explores shared consciousness and what

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:18.160
happens to a human body after it dies.

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:21.120
And this show springs out into what is reality

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:26.159
and how do we interface with it. Some of
the goal is to, sort of, get the audience

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:29.060
to question what is real.

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:31.300
We concentrate more on making you feel a thing or

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:35.430
perceiving your own perceptions, or
seeing yourself see. Like realizing that

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:39.780
you are a being who has perceptions, or
realizing that you are a mind who thinks.

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:43.060
What we try to do is use technology in

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:44.940
service of manifesting humanity, I would say.

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:49.170
To expose the technology then strip
it away and then be left with human

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:50.840
beings in a room together.

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:52.980
This artist in residency program here

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:54.680
sort of gives us

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:59.060
this major advantage over the other
things that are out there, because not

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:03.450
only do we get to bring in artists that
we think are really important in the

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:07.619
current contemporary world, we get to
expose our students to that kind of work

00:02:07.619 --> 00:02:11.489
and they get to experience it first-hand.
So even when we see a show at another

00:02:11.489 --> 00:02:14.670
institution, we see the the facade of the show but we

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:17.040
don't get to go backstage and really find
out how it works.

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:23.400
He was using tools that

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:29.069
are are really not that different than
what other theater artists are using, but

00:02:29.069 --> 00:02:31.380
he's using them in sort of a new and
exciting way.

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:32.660
So this is, I guess, my way

00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:38.960
of actually interfacing with a mass of
people and sort of saying "hey, right?"

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:42.080
"Does anyone else experience life like this?"

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:45.080
"And doesn't it feel like this?"

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:49.720
And that's what I think theatre, in it's
essence, actually does

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:52.400
is say those same things.

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:53.730
And I think that I
just happen to go about it in a

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:55.480
different way.

